  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\n07z/2am surface analysis shows a 1032mb high anchored over\nOntario/Quebec while a stationary frontal boundary is draped\nfrom the southern Appalachians to Texas. Despite synoptic\nsubsidence and a drying E/NE flow from the Great Lakes, ample\ncloud cover remains locked in place across the Midwest early\nthis morning. The latest IR satellite imagery shows a few breaks\nin the low overcast...most notably along/just south of I-70 and\nalong I-55 from Chicago to Lincoln. Given continued E/NE flow, \nmost CAMs show widespread cloud cover upstream across Indiana \nspilling back westward across much of central Illinois as the \nmorning progresses. The exception will be along/west of I-55 \nwhere partial clearing appears likely. As an upper-level low \nevident on water vapor imagery over eastern Iowa slowly tracks \neastward, a few very light showers or sprinkles will be \npossible. While the NAM suggests MUCAPEs will remain below \n100J/kg, think this may be a bit underdone given the expected \nbreaks in the overcast. With sufficient moisture/forcing and \nRRFS 700-500mb lapse rates of around 6-7C/km, have mentioned \nisolated showers/sprinkles along/north of the I-72 corridor \nthrough the afternoon. High temperatures will mostly be in the \nmiddle to upper 60s, with lower 70s along/south of I-70.\n\nWhile surface ridging will build into the region tonight,\npersistent boundary layer moisture beneath the subsidence\ninversion will create a challenging cloud cover forecast. While\nprevious thinking suggested partly to mostly clear skies and\ngood radiational cooling conditions overnight, the latest data\nindicates the low cloud cover remaining in place and/or\nspreading back westward across much of the KILX CWA. As a\nresult, think partial clearing will be limited to the Illinois\nRiver Valley with cloudy skies further east. Due to the \nincreased cloud cover, have raised low temperatures by a few \ndegrees...ranging from the lower 50s northwest of the Illinois \nRiver to the upper 50s along/east of I-57.\n\nMostly cloudy conditions with below normal temperatures in the\nupper 60s and lower 70s will continue into Wednesday before\nincreasing amounts of sunshine slowly push readings back into\nthe lower to middle 70s by Friday/Saturday. After that, a\nblocking pattern will develop across the eastern CONUS featuring\na deep upper low over the Mid-Atlantic region and a ridge of\nhigh pressure further west across the Midwest. As a result, it\nappears a return to above normal temperatures in the 80s is in\nstore for central Illinois by early next week.",
